          %h2#idea Idea
          %blockquote
            %p
              Der unendlich kleinste Theil des Raumes ist immer ein Raum, etwas, das Continuität hat, nicht aber ein blosser Punct, oder die Grenze zwischen bestimmten Stellen im Raume; (
              %a{:href => "Grundriss+des+Eigenthümlichen+der+Wissenschaftslehre#4IVUnendlichKleinsterTeilDesRaumes"}> Fichte 1795, Grundriss, §4.IV
              )
          %p
            In
            %em synthetic differential geometry
            one formulates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatically
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            – called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+toposes"} smooth toposes
            – of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+spaces"} generalized smooth spaces
            by assuming the explicit existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhoods"} infinitesimal neighbourhoods
            of points.
          %p
            The main point of the axioms is to ensure that a well defined notion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+spaces"} infinitesimal spaces
            exists in the topos, whose existence concretely and usefully formalizes the wide-spread but often vague intuition about the role of infinitesimals in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %p
            In particular, in such toposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            there exists an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            that behaves like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal interval
            in such a way that for any space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in E
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext :=
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X \;\text{:=}\; X^D
            (using the notation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential objects
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %em infinitesimal path
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This way, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth toposes
            it is possible to give precise well-defined meaning to many of the familiar computations – wide-spread in particular in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            literature – that compute with supposedly “infinitesimal” quantities.
          #SophusLieQuote.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              As quoted by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"} Anders Kock
              in his
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://users-math.au.dk/kock/sdg99.pdf#page=9"} first book (p. 9)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sophus+Lie"} Sophus Lie
              – one of the founding fathers of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
              and, of course
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
              – once said that he found his main theorems in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
              using “synthetic reasoning”, but had to write them up in non-synthetic style (see
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+versus+synthetic"} analytic versus synthetic
            %blockquote
              %p
                “The reason why I have postponed for so long these investigations, which are basic to my other work in this field, is essentially the following: I found these theories originally by synthetic considerations. But I soon realized that, as expedient (
                %em zweckmässig
                ) the synthetic method is for discovery, as difficult it is to give a clear exposition on synthetic investigations, which lead to deal with objects that till now have almost exclusively been considered analytically. After long vacillations, I have decided to use a half synthetic, half analytic form. I hope my work will serve to bring justification to the synthetic method besides the analytical one.” (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sophus+Lie"}> Sophus Lie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Allgemeine Theorie der partiellen Differentialgleichungen erster Ordnung
          %p Synthetic differential geometry provides this formalized language.
          #Peirce.num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Another advocate of the use of infinitesimals in the late 19th century was the American philosopher
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Sanders+Peirce"} Charles Sanders Peirce
            %blockquote
              %p
                The illumination of the subject by a strict notation for the logic of relatives had shown me clearly and evidently that the idea of an infinitesimal involves no contradiction…As a mathematician, I prefer the method of infinitesimals to that of limits, as far easier and less infested with snares. Charles Sanders Peirce,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Law of Mind
                %strong 2
                (1892)
            %p
              According to Bell (
              %a{:href => "#Bell98"}> 1998, p.5
              ):
            %blockquote
              %p Peirce was aware, even before Brouwer, that a faithful account of the truly continuous will involve jettisoning the law of excluded(.)
          %h2#axiomatics Axiomatics
          %p
            The axioms of synthetic differential geometry demand that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            of smooth spaces is
          %ul
            %li
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
              (see there for details)
          %p in which in particular
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal spaces
                exist and
            %li
              %p
                satisfy the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
          %p Depending on applications one imposes further axioms, such as the
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+axiom"}> integration axiom
              \.
          %p
            With that little bit of axiomatics alone, a large amount of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            may be formulated. This has been carried through quite comprehensively by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anders+Kock"} Anders Kock
            %a{:href => "#References"} reference
            below.
          %p
            In his work he particularly makes use of the fact that as sophisticated as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            may be when explicitly constructed (see the section on
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#models"} models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            means that one can reason inside it almost literally as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kock-Lawvere+axiom"} Kock-Lawvere axiom
            is satisfied for “numbers”. Here “numbers” is really to be interpreted in the topos, but if one just accepts that they satisfy the KL axiom, one may work with infinitesimals in this context in essentially precisely the naive way, with the topos theory in the background just ensuring that everything makes good sense.
          %h2#models Models
          %p
            Being axiomatic, reasoning in synthetic differential geometry applies in every
            %strong model
            for the axioms, i.e. in every concrete choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Models of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+toposes"} smooth toposes
            tend to be inspired by, but more general than, constructions familiar from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            in his work, that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+ideals"} nilpotent ideals
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            are formal duals of spaces with infinitesimal extension is typically used to model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+spaces"} infinitesimal spaces
            in synthetic differential geometry.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry+applied+to+algebraic+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry applied to algebraic geometry
            for more on this.
          %p
            The main difference between models for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+toposes"} smooth toposes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            from this perspective is that models for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            tend to employ test spaces that are
            %em richer
            than plain formal duals to commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} rings
            or algebras, as in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+algebras"} generalized smooth algebras
            that remember “smooth structure” in the usual sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (and different from, though not entirely unrelated to, the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth scheme
            in algebraic geometry). This is in particular true for the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#wellAdaptedModels"} well adapted models
          %p
            However, with a a sufficiently general perspective on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            one finds that algebraic geometry and synthetic differential geometry are both special cases of a more general notion of theories of generalized spaces. For more on this see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+scheme"} generalized scheme
          %h3#wellAdaptedModels Well adapted models
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            modelling the axioms of synthetic differential geometry is called
            %strong (well) adapted
            if the ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            embeds into it, in particular if there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to E
            from the category of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifolds
            into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            A standard model for well adapted synthetic toposes is obtained in terms of sheaves on duals of “germ determined”
            = succeed "-rings." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
          %p
            The conception and discussion of these well adapted toposes goes back to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Dubuc"} Eduardo Dubuc
            %a{:href => "http://north.ecc.edu/alsani/ct99-00%288-12%29/msg00218.html"} asks
            people to refer to this topos as the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dubuc+topos"} Dubuc topos
          %p This theory of well-adapted models was later summarized and extended in the standard textbook
          %ul
            %li#MoerdijkReyes1991
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Models+for+Smooth+Infinitesimal+Analysis"} Models for Smooth Infinitesimal Analysis
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-4143-8"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4143-8
          %h2#variations Variations
          %h3#higher_categorical_versions Higher categorical versions
          %ul
            %li
              Synthetic differential geometry may be thought of as embedded in the general theory of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+smooth+manifolds"} derived smooth manifolds
              and, generally, that of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+schemes"} generalized schemes
          %h3#supergeometric_versions Supergeometric versions
          %p
            The notion of synthetic differential geometry extends to the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry+-+contents"} supergeometry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+supergeometry"}> synthetic differential supergeometry
              \.
          %h2#Constructions Constructions in synthetic differential geometry
          %h3#tangent_bundle Tangent bundle
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X := X^D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {*} \to D
            induces a canonical projection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X \to X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to T X
            of that projection is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector+field"} tangent vector field
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunct"} adjunct
            is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D \to X^X
            that sends the unique point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            to the identity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_X \in X^X
          %h3#differential_equation Differential equation
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            is an extension problem in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            along a morphism that includes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal object
            into another object.
          %p
            For instance the ordinary first order homogeneous differential equation that asks the derivative of a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to A
            along a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v : D \to X^X
            to be given by a specified map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: X \to T A
            is given by a diagram of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi α
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi f
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi A
                            %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  D &amp;\stackrel{v}{\to}&amp; X^X
                  \\\
                  {}^{\mathllap{\alpha}}\downarrow &amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{f}}
                  \\\
                  A^X
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where we have freely identified morphisms with their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunct"} adjuncts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+equation"} differential equation
            for details.
          %h3#differential_forms Differential forms
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 1-form
            is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega : T X \to R
            that is “fiberwise linear”. One elegant way to say this is obtained by considering all higher differential forms at once:
          %p
            for a sufficiently well behaved object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+topos"} smooth topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+singular+simplicial+complex"} infinitesimal singular simplicial complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi inf
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{(\Delta^\bullet_{inf})}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+algebra"} cosimplicial algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi inf
                          %mo •
                    %mo ,
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(X^{\Delta^\bullet_{inf}}, R)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} normalized Moore cochain complex
            is isomorphic to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+complex"} de Rham dg-algebra
            of differential forms on
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi N
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                        %mi inf
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi dR
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N^\bullet(Hom(X^{(\Delta^{inf}_\bullet)},R))
                  \=
                  \\Omega^\bullet_{dR}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p This is discussed at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object#SpacOfInfSimpl"} Spaces of infinitesimal k-simplices
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms+in+synthetic+differential+geometry"}> differential forms in synthetic differential geometry
                \.
